<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">al-Mu'tadid</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 902</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as al-Mu'tadid bi'llah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Mu'tadid was Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> from 892 to 902. As the son of the regent al-Muwaffaq he helped his father crush the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanj">Zanj</a> rebellion in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> in the 880s. As caliph he restored the authority of the caliphate, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> the permanent seat of the caliphate again, ended the rule of the Dulafids in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jibal">Jibal</a> and married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qatr-al-nada">Qatr al-Nada</a>, daughter of the Tulunid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khumarawayh">Khumarawayh</a>. His reign began a short Abbasid recovery.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-mutadid.jpg" alt="al-Mu'tadid" width="320" height="153" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dinar_of_al-Mu%27tadid,_AH_285.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
